= Dashboard on Raspberry Pi
On the IIoT Rack (@fig:iiot-rack:hardware) you also have a Raspberry Pi on bottom.
This Raspberry already run another dashboard.
You will now map the MAC address of the Raspberry Pi to a fixed IP address on the router.
You will then access the dashboard on the Raspberry Pi with this IP and a name.

#task[Map RPI MAC to IP][
  Now your Raspberry Pi have an IP by the DHCP server on the router.
  You will now map the MAC address of the Raspberry Pi to #raspberry-pi-ip.

  #subtask[
    Find the MAC address of the Raspberry Pi and it's actual IP address.
  ]

  #subtask[
    Set #raspberry-pi-ip for the Raspberry Pi through a static DHCP mapping on the router.
    #infobox[
      The menu in the Ubiquiti router can be found under:\
      `Services` $->$ `DHCP Server` $->$ `Configure Static Map`
      ]
  ]

  #subtask[
    Show with Wireshark the `http` traffic between your laptop and the Raspberry Pi. 
  ]
]

#pagebreak()

#task[DNS for webpage on RPI][
  Since you now have a fixed IP address for the Raspberry Pi, you can now access it with a name. We already configure a DNS server on the router with a hostname for #raspberry-pi-ip. You can now access the dashboard on the Raspberry Pi with the name #raspberry-pi-dns-name. You will now analyse the traffic with Wireshark and write a small report about it.

  #subtask[
    Verify you have access to the dashboard as before but now with #raspberry-pi-dns-name.
  ]

  #subtask[
    Observe the DNS resolution and the traffic with Wireshark. Show and explain what you see with your words on your report.
    #infobox[
      You can force to use a specific DNS server with nslookup and avoiding to use your cache \
      e.g.: `nslookup dashboard.lan 192.168.1.1` to force using `192.168.1.1` DNS server to resolve `sahboard.lan`.
    ]
  ]
]
